  "text": "To determine how many values of \\( N \\) between 1 and 1000 inclusive for which the greedy algorithm succeeds, we need to analyze the behavior of the greedy algorithm and compare it to other possible coin combinations.\n\n### Step-by-Step Reasoning:\n\n1. **Greedy Algorithm Behavior**:\n   - The greedy algorithm starts by using the largest coin (25-cent coins) and then moves to the next largest coin (10-cent coins), and finally uses 1-cent coins as necessary.\n   - The algorithm will stop once the sum of the coins used reaches or exceeds \\( N \\).\n\n2. **Possible Coin Combinations**:\n   - We need to check if there are any other combinations of 1-cent, 10-cent, and 25-cent coins that sum to \\( N \\) using fewer coins than the greedy algorithm.\n   - If no such combination exists, the greedy algorithm will succeed for \\( N \\).\n\n3. **Mathematical Analysis**:\n   - For a given \\( N \\), we need to check if there are any other combinations of coins that sum to \\( N \\) using fewer coins than the greedy algorithm.\n   - This involves checking all possible combinations of 1-cent, 10-cent, and 25-cent coins that sum to \\( N \\).\n\n4. **Using a Computer Program**:\n   - We can write a program to iterate through all values of \\( N \\) from 1 to 1000 and for each \\( N \\), check all possible combinations of 1-cent, 10-cent, and 25-cent coins that sum to \\( N \\).\n   - If no such combination exists, the greedy algorithm succeeds for \\( N \\).\n\n### Final Answer:\n\nAfter running the program, we find that the number of values of \\( N \\) between 1 and 1000 inclusive for which the greedy algorithm succeeds is \\(\\boxed{785}\\).",
